        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Check Laser Level Accuracy",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/how-to-check-laser-level-accuracy/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I know if my laser level is accurate?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Run a two-wall test. Mark the cross on two walls with the laser at the midpoint, then again with the laser close to one wall, and compare the distances between the pairs of marks. If the difference is within the laser's rated accuracy for that distance, the laser is accurate."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should you check laser level accuracy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Check it whenever the laser has been dropped or knocked, before any accuracy-critical job such as tile or cabinets, and periodically for frequently used tools. Many professionals check monthly or before each major job. It only takes about twenty minutes once you know the routine."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a good accuracy for a laser level?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Plus or minus 1/8 inch at 30 feet is common and accurate enough for most interior work. Better models quote tighter figures, such as 1/16 inch at 30 feet. Rotary lasers for site work are often rated even more precisely over longer distances."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a laser level lose calibration?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Drops, impacts and rough transport can shift the pendulum or optics, so the laser no longer projects a level line even though it looks normal. Always engage the transport lock when moving the laser, and check accuracy after any knock."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I calibrate a laser level myself?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Some lasers have a user calibration mode described in the manual. If yours does, follow the procedure exactly. If it does not, send it to the manufacturer or an authorized service center rather than opening the housing or adjusting internal parts yourself."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do you check if a laser level is plumb?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Hang a plumb bob on a long string and align the laser's vertical line with the string at the top. Any gap between line and string at the bottom shows the plumb error. You can also flip the laser around a door frame and compare marks."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does my laser level give different readings each time?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Inconsistent readings usually point to a mount that is moving, a laser that has not finished settling, weak batteries, or a damaged pendulum or damping system. Rule out the simple causes first, then have the laser serviced if the results remain inconsistent."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
