          <a href="#tilt" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The Line Tilt Check</a>
          <a href="#plumb" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. The Plumb Check</a>
          <a href="#results" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Reading the Results</a>
          <a href="#fix" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. What to Do If It Fails</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Every laser level leaves the factory calibrated to a stated accuracy, usually something like plus or minus 1/8 inch at 30 feet. A drop off a ladder, a rough ride in a truck bed, or years of use can shift that without any visible sign. The line still looks crisp and straight, but it is no longer level. You can check accuracy yourself in about twenty minutes with two walls, a pencil and a tape measure, and you should do it before any job where a small error multiplies, such as tile or long runs of cabinets.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="spec" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What the Accuracy Spec Actually Means
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laser level accuracy is usually expressed as a maximum deviation over a distance, such as plus or minus 1/8 inch at 30 feet, or plus or minus 3 millimeters at 10 meters. Some brands state it as millimeters per meter or as an angle in arc-seconds. They all describe the same thing: how far the beam may point above or below true level, scaled by distance from the laser.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because it is an angle, error grows in a straight line with distance. A laser rated at 1/8 inch at 30 feet may be off by up to 1/16 inch at 15 feet and 1/4 inch at 60 feet. That is why a laser that seems fine on a short wall can cause visible problems on a long kitchen run or across a large room. It also means you need a reasonable distance, ideally 20 feet or more, to test accuracy meaningfully.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Distance from Laser</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Allowed Error at 1/8 in per 30 ft</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Allowed Error at 1/16 in per 30 ft</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">10 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about 1/24 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about 1/48 in</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">15 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/16 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/32 in</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">30 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/8 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/16 in</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">60 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/4 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/8 in</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Find the accuracy figure for your specific laser in its manual or on the manufacturer's website before you start. You will need it to judge the result. Many manuals also include their own field calibration procedure, which may differ slightly from the general method below. Where the manual gives an allowance for its own test, use that number. The <a href="/best-laser-level/">laser levels we recommend</a> all publish their accuracy spec.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Before You Test
          </h2>

          <p>
            Find two parallel walls about 20 to 30 feet apart, such as the ends of a garage, a long hallway, or opposite walls of a large room. The longer the distance, the easier it is to see a small error. You will be marking on both walls, so painter's tape on each wall gives you a clean surface to mark and remove afterwards.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use a stable tripod or a solid, level surface. Any movement of the laser between marks will ruin the test. Put fresh batteries in, release the transport lock, and let the laser warm up to room temperature for a few minutes if it has been in a cold vehicle. Wait until the line is completely still before each mark.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use a sharp pencil and always mark the same part of the line, ideally the center. The line has width, and marking the top edge on one wall and the center on the other introduces error that has nothing to do with the laser. Many people find it helps to mark a short horizontal tick through the center of the line and a vertical tick where the cross is.
          </p>

          <p>
            Make sure the laser is in self-leveling mode, not manual lock mode, and that it is not flashing an out-of-level warning. If you are not sure about the modes on your tool, our explanation of <a href="/self-leveling-vs-manual-laser-level/">self-leveling vs manual lasers</a> covers what each one does. Testing a laser that is in lock mode tells you nothing useful.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="two-wall" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Two-Wall Peg Test for Level
          </h2>

          <p>
            The two-wall test, sometimes called the peg test, checks whether the horizontal beam is truly level. It works because placing the laser exactly halfway between the walls cancels out any error, giving you a true level reference to compare against. Here is the procedure for a cross-line laser.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Step 1.</strong> Set the laser exactly halfway between the two walls, with the cross pointing at wall A. Let it level, then mark the center of the cross on wall A. Call this mark A1. <strong>Step 2.</strong> Without changing the height, rotate the laser 180 degrees so it points at wall B. Mark the cross on wall B and call it B1. Because the laser was at the midpoint, any tilt raised or lowered both marks by the same amount, so A1 and B1 are at the same true level.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Step 3.</strong> Move the laser close to wall A, about a foot or two away, and set it to the same height if possible. Point it at wall A and mark A2. <strong>Step 4.</strong> Rotate it 180 degrees toward wall B and mark B2. <strong>Step 5.</strong> Measure the vertical distance between A1 and A2, and between B1 and B2. If the laser is perfectly level, those two distances will be the same.
          </p>

          <p>
            The difference between those two distances is your error over the distance between the laser's two positions. For example, if A1 to A2 is 1/2 inch and B1 to B2 is 5/8 inch, the difference is 1/8 inch over roughly the 28 feet between the laser's positions. Compare that with your laser's spec scaled to that distance. Repeat the test with the laser turned 90 degrees to check the other horizontal axis, since a laser can be accurate in one direction and off in another.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE KEY IDEA</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">The midpoint gives you true level</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">When the laser sits exactly halfway between the walls, any tilt shifts both marks equally, so they are level with each other. Moving the laser close to one wall then reveals the tilt as a difference between the two walls.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="tilt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Line Tilt Check
          </h2>

          <p>
            A laser can be level at the center of the cross but have a horizontal line that slopes slightly from one end to the other. This matters for tile and cabinets, where you mark along the length of the line rather than at the cross. The line tilt check catches it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set the laser about 15 to 20 feet from a long wall and project the horizontal line onto it. Mark the center of the line directly in front of the laser. Then mark the line about 6 to 8 feet to the left and the same distance to the right, using the center of the line each time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Now rotate the laser on its base so the left end of the line moves to where the center mark was, and check whether the line still passes through that mark. Repeat for the right side. Alternatively, connect the outer marks with a long straightedge or chalk line and compare them to a known level reference. If the line passes through all marks, the fan is level along its length.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the line consistently rises toward one end, the diode or lens may be slightly rotated in its housing. Compare the deviation with your manual, which sometimes gives a separate tolerance for line tilt. This check is quick once the laser is set up for the two-wall test, so it is worth doing both at once.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="plumb" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Plumb Check
          </h2>

          <p>
            The vertical line needs checking separately. The simplest reference is a plumb bob. Hang one from a high point, such as a ceiling hook or the top of a door frame, with a string of at least 8 feet, and let it settle completely. A plumb bob always hangs truly vertical, so it is an ideal reference.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set the laser a few feet from the string and project the vertical line onto it. Align the line with the string at the top, then check where the line falls relative to the string at the bottom. Any gap between the line and the string at the bottom shows how far the vertical line is out of plumb over that height.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do not have a plumb bob, use a door frame. Project the vertical line onto the wall about a foot from the frame and mark the line at the top and bottom. Rotate the laser 180 degrees, move it to the other side of the room so the line falls on the same marks, and mark it again. If the vertical line is plumb, the two sets of marks will line up; if not, the gap is twice the error.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many cross-line lasers also project plumb dots up and down. You can check them by marking the down dot on the floor, then the up dot on the ceiling, rotating the laser 180 degrees and checking whether both dots return to the same marks. Plumb dots are useful for transferring layout, as described in our guide on <a href="/how-to-use-a-laser-level/">how to use a laser level</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="results" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Reading the Results
          </h2>

          <p>
            Write down every measurement as you go. Small fractions are easy to misremember, and you want to compare the error to the spec precisely. Measuring in millimeters can make the arithmetic easier if your tape has both scales.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the measured error is within the laser's rated accuracy for that distance, the laser passes, and you can keep using it with confidence. Remember that the spec is a maximum, so a result that is close to the limit is still a pass, though it is worth retesting after any further knocks.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the error is slightly over the spec, repeat the whole test carefully. Marking errors, a tripod that shifted, or a laser that had not fully settled can all produce a false fail. Consistent results across two or three attempts are what count.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the error is well over the spec, or the test results are inconsistent each time, the laser needs attention. A laser that gives a different answer every time may have a damaged pendulum or damping system. Either way, do not use it for accuracy-critical work until it is fixed. A good <a href="/best-tile-leveling-system/">tile leveling system</a> can keep tiles flat to each other, but it cannot fix a first course set to a crooked line.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Field Check</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20-30 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Distance between walls</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2 axes</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Test level both ways</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~20 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Time for all checks</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Repeat a borderline result</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="fix" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What to Do If Your Laser Fails
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the simple causes. Replace the batteries, make sure the transport lock is fully released, and confirm the laser is not in manual mode. Check that the tripod head is tight and that the laser is seated firmly on the mount. Then repeat the test.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some lasers allow user calibration. The manual will explain how, usually by entering a calibration mode and adjusting the beam against a reference following a specific procedure. Follow it exactly. Do not open the housing or try to adjust internal screws on a laser that does not support user calibration, because you can easily make it worse and may void the warranty.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your laser does not support user calibration, or you are not confident doing it, send it to the manufacturer or an authorized service center. Many major brands offer calibration service, and some cover it under warranty for a period. For an inexpensive laser, calibration service may cost a significant fraction of a replacement, so compare costs before sending it.
          </p>

          <p>
            To keep a laser accurate, always engage the transport lock before moving it, store it in its case, and avoid drops. Check it whenever it has been knocked, and before any job where precision matters. If you are replacing a laser that keeps failing, our <a href="/best-laser-level/">laser level buying guide</a> highlights models that hold calibration well, and our <a href="/green-vs-red-laser-level/">green vs red comparison</a> helps you pick the right beam.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing over a short distance</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A small angular error is almost invisible over 6 or 8 feet. Testing across a short wall can make a faulty laser look perfect. Use two walls at least 20 feet apart so the error grows large enough to measure with a tape.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not placing the laser at the midpoint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The two-wall test only works if the first set of marks is made with the laser exactly halfway between the walls. If it is closer to one wall, the reference marks are not truly level, and the result will be misleading.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Marking different parts of the line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The laser line has width. If you mark the top edge on one wall and the center on another, you add error that has nothing to do with the laser. Always mark the center of the line or cross.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Only testing one axis</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A laser can be accurate when pointed one way and off when turned 90 degrees. Repeat the two-wall test on both horizontal axes, and check the vertical line separately with a plumb bob or door frame method.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adjusting a laser that is not user-calibratable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Opening the housing or turning internal screws on a laser that does not support field calibration usually makes things worse and may void the warranty. Send it to the manufacturer or an authorized service center instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my laser level is accurate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Run a two-wall test. Mark the cross on two walls with the laser at the midpoint, then again with the laser close to one wall, and compare the distances between the pairs of marks. If the difference is within the laser's rated accuracy for that distance, the laser is accurate.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you check laser level accuracy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check it whenever the laser has been dropped or knocked, before any accuracy-critical job such as tile or cabinets, and periodically for frequently used tools. Many professionals check monthly or before each major job. It only takes about twenty minutes once you know the routine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good accuracy for a laser level?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plus or minus 1/8 inch at 30 feet is common and accurate enough for most interior work. Better models quote tighter figures, such as 1/16 inch at 30 feet. Rotary lasers for site work are often rated even more precisely over longer distances.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a laser level lose calibration?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Drops, impacts and rough transport can shift the pendulum or optics, so the laser no longer projects a level line even though it looks normal. Always engage the transport lock when moving the laser, and check accuracy after any knock.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I calibrate a laser level myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some lasers have a user calibration mode described in the manual. If yours does, follow the procedure exactly. If it does not, send it to the manufacturer or an authorized service center rather than opening the housing or adjusting internal parts yourself.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you check if a laser level is plumb?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hang a plumb bob on a long string and align the laser's vertical line with the string at the top. Any gap between line and string at the bottom shows the plumb error. You can also flip the laser around a door frame and compare marks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my laser level give different readings each time?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Inconsistent readings usually point to a mount that is moving, a laser that has not finished settling, weak batteries, or a damaged pendulum or damping system. Rule out the simple causes first, then have the laser serviced if the results remain inconsistent.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

